    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and both its tests pass along with a clean `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the registration-date behavior for `POST /api/owners` so a supplied date is kept and an omitted one defaults to the server's current date, returned in ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` format.\n\nChanges, each kept in the unit where it belongs:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added an optional `registrationDate` field (`type: string, format: date`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. Because `Owner` composes `OwnerFields`, this single addition makes the field bind on the way in (request DTO) and serialize on the way out (response DTO) as ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`. MapStruct maps it by name in both directions, so no mapper changes were needed.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `LocalDate registrationDate` column with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`DefaultOwnerRegistrationDate.java`** (new function) \u2014 a single-responsibility step that sets the registration date to `LocalDate.now()` only when the built owner has none, leaving a supplied date untouched.\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step into the pipeline between `build` and `save` (under the same `transaction` governance), so both the persisted row and the response reflect the value.\n\n5. **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added a nullable `registration_date DATE` column. The hsqldb schema is what the tests use (`ddl-auto: none`), so it was required there; the others were updated for consistency.\n\nI deliberately avoided touching `BuildOwner` or the mapper (which would have made them catch-alls) and instead added a focused defaulting step, reusing the existing DTO/entity/mapper plumbing.",
